<section class="block-section story">
  <span class="section-tag">Story</span>
  <h2>What today is about</h2>
  <p class="sub">The unit project starts today. Everything in Blocks 1 through 3 was preparation for it.</p>
  <p>Here is the whole thing: <strong>pick one contested scientific topic, find three sources of different kinds, OPVL each one, work out where they disagree and why, and defend your evaluation in a five-minute talk.</strong> That is the <a href="/units/unit-0/assessment-source-dossier/">Source Dossier</a>, the Unit 0 summative. It is due Block 6 and defended Block 7.</p>
  <p>You already have every skill it needs. Block 1 gave you two texts on one flood that did not agree. Block 2 put you inside a real 1965 paper. Block 3 showed you three AI responses to that paper, one of which confidently named a chemical Lovelock never wrote down. The dossier just asks you to do all of that on a question you picked yourself.</p>
  <p>Today is about <strong>choosing well</strong>, because a badly chosen topic makes the next three blocks miserable. You will test candidate topics against one standard: do serious sources actually disagree about this? Then you lock it with Ms. Jayanthi/Mr. Matt and start hunting your first source.</p>
  <p>"Traditional" in the source list means "not generative AI": a textbook chapter, a news article, an interview, a peer-reviewed paper, a primary document. You OPVL two of those and one AI source. Separating them is deliberate, so you can feel what OPVL reveals about each type. The same four questions; very different answers.</p>
</section>

