<section class="block-section do">
  <span class="section-tag">Do</span>
  <h2>Today's work: choose one path</h2>
  <p class="sub">Topic conferences with Ms. Jayanthi/Mr. Matt run all block; come to the desk when you are ready. Everything else is source-hunting. The path you pick is about how settled your topic already is.</p>
  <figure class="content-figure">
    <img src="/assets/img/u0/u0-dossier-pipeline.svg" alt="Flow diagram of the Source Dossier: three sources (traditional, weaker, and AI) each get OPVL, flow into a synthesis, then a defense with a five-minute talk and two-minute Q&amp;A; an AI Documentation Template rail runs alongside recording what the AI did, what you did, and one thing it got wrong; timeline markers show Blocks 4 through 7.">
    <figcaption>The whole project on one page. Find where you are today.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="choice-intro">
    <strong>Learning Intention:</strong> {{ learningIntention | safe }}
  </div>
  <div class="choice-board">
    <div class="choice">
      <div class="choice-letter">A</div>
      <div class="choice-title">Decide between two or three candidates</div>
      <div class="choice-detail">You have a shortlist but no winner. For each candidate, spend ten minutes finding out whether two serious sources actually disagree. The one where you find real disagreement fastest is your topic. Lock it, then start source one.</div>
      <span class="udl-tag">Solo · Default</span>
    </div>
    <div class="choice">
      <div class="choice-letter">B</div>
      <div class="choice-title">Topic settled, go straight to sources</div>
      <div class="choice-detail">If your topic is already clear to you, lock it early and spend the block hunting. Find your traditional reputable source and your weaker source, and get the first OPVL properly started. Aim for sources that take genuinely different angles, not two versions of the same take.</div>
      <span class="udl-tag">Solo · Stretch</span>
    </div>
    <div class="choice">
      <div class="choice-letter">C</div>
      <div class="choice-title">No idea where to start</div>
      <div class="choice-detail">Open <a href="/units/unit-0/contested-topics/">the topic list</a> and pick the three that annoy, worry, or interest you most. Read one short thing on each. Choose the one you find yourself arguing with. Interest survives four blocks of work; obligation does not. Every topic there comes with a note on why it is contested and a first place to look.</div>
      <span class="udl-tag">Solo · Choice</span>
    </div>
    <div class="choice">
      <div class="choice-letter">D</div>
      <div class="choice-title">Topic-testing station</div>
      <div class="choice-detail">Not sure whether your topic is genuinely contested, or arrived with nothing at all? Come to the table, and bring <a href="/units/unit-0/contested-topics/">the topic list</a> if you have not chosen yet. Say it out loud and let two other students try to argue the other side. If nobody can, you have your answer and you save yourself three blocks of trouble. Ms. Jayanthi/Mr. Matt floats. Capacity ~6 at a time.</div>
      <span class="udl-tag">Pairs · Practice</span>
    </div>
  </div>
